71 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $1\\times4$ matrix $A$ has at most $1$ singular value.\n\n- Any $4\\times2$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- The matrix ![](images/b8/b8601b1e66ef1d302e7194f262f6a513063bba2114fb063c2fc16542d4684392.svg) has one negative singular value." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcd3c46b-a87f-5ca8-9cf5-d9444a9c8218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184095 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $1 \\times 4$ matrix and $A^T$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, $A^T \\! A$ must be a $4 \\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4} \\times {\\large 1}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 1} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4}}{A} =  \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix and $A^T$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, $A^T \\! A$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 4}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 4} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ cannot have a negative singular value.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184096 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184096 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/85/85f9ccea6cd39f07174651d07316218edf50041f9d373e22cdaedf0c779caaf3.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b9ef509-4165-5772-81c1-9a3d78424dd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184096 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184097 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184097 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/59/590d5dc32950efa0fce63a5d64a5595210a3ccb08c613e364586dba4822d7314.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184097 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fc137a7-9f7a-58de-ac92-1c7c2bdd53f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184097 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )^{2}-(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184197 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184198 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184199 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing the Singular Values of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186184968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184404 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d3825ee6-b390-5321-ae3c-5fb0645c7b7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184880 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184880 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184880 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n- Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ has two non-negative singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184880 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca5e4c86-b607-523c-a857-24250b844373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184880 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $10\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 10$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 10}\\times {A}_{10\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1061 30545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6870e18-6c9b-57a7-81ca-e24f2af2e69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184968 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1061 30988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92a4b43a-dde9-5e42-8877-3e32ba9082f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184970 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1061 30989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "551770c2-906e-5338-b743-2d2902903e74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184971 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1061 30990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "749703b1-407a-5adb-8fda-1176ec5284b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184972 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/a9/a9edbe1fee59758c9557d95ea8f62468f9f3cb98449c437a274bdbf5c30f7dc6.svg)\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$  must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1065 4891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6230f35e-b526-5e84-be30-86c8cdcbd301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185322 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367540 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7d5c241-f1dd-539c-bc82-e319298f04d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367540 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}13-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 12 & 13-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (13-\\lambda )(13-\\lambda )-144 & =0 \\\\ 169-26\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-26\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186367544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367541 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2b11b93b-5ed4-5c27-8e1f-ae8a252505fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367542 #edb/ref 1080 "$5$ and $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11f72538-1df2-5400-9f78-cb8409f95ed7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367543 #edb/ref 1080 "$4$ and $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367543 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367543 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aaed5f82-08d1-5931-9dc0-ef6782cb00fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367544 #edb/ref 1080 "$5$ and $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367544 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367544 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "85459f08-ebd2-5de2-af01-144d2aeb100c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367545 #edb/ref 1080 "$5$ and $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367545 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367545 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d4be0251-97f5-5981-bda6-e62b677f0ba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367546 #edb/ref 1080 "$4$ and $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7dc7fb6-4c25-5dc1-b337-7a9abd5eaf65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367547 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40fb180a-20af-5d07-ba44-09c0a1ca7e05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367547 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda [(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4)] & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda ({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{0}=0 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186367552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367548 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1d25d269-9cbc-53c8-af16-a9f6f0118125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367549 #edb/ref 1080 "5,0,0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "547cd0d6-fd5f-5b94-99cd-d4762af1fc66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367550 #edb/ref 1080 "25,5,0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367550 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367550 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "427695ad-2a8a-55af-9742-265b5d33144e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367551 #edb/ref 1080 "5,5,0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367551 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367551 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49754909-9c5a-50e2-bda8-1f655927811b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367552 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{5},0,0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367552 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367552 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b92ac663-8b9a-5f7f-855b-b8691c3e6c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367553 #edb/ref 1080 "5,1,0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367553 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "edca49b3-568f-5e5b-91e9-bd57fa31411b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368899 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any matrix of the form  $A=[\\begin{aligned}-a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $5\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n- Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e856448-81c6-5a27-b63c-c2864d6eca93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368899 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, \n$A$  can't have any negative singular value.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed,  since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368903 #edb/ref 17592